{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: forecasts are systematically too high by ~0.3–1.5 EUR for the first ~1–15 days when prices are stable/ranging; apply a small downward level shift early-horizon.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the market transitions into a strong uptrend (as in the late part of the examples), the model fails to follow the breakout and becomes too low; add a trend-following uplift when recent returns/MA slope are strongly positive.",
    "Horizon asymmetry: short-horizon (days 1–10) tends to be high-biased, while longer horizon (days ~20–30) tends to be low-biased in rising regimes; use a horizon-dependent bias correction that tilts downward early and upward late when momentum is positive.",
    "Mean-reversion overshoot: in non-trending periods the model projects persistence at elevated levels and does not revert enough (stays above realized); increase pull-to-mean when recent volatility is low and momentum is flat.",
    "Breakout/spike handling: after a price surge, the model often assumes partial retracement while the true series continues higher (missed continuation); if the last 5–10 days show consecutive higher highs/higher lows, reduce any assumed retracement and raise the tail of the forecast path."
  ]
